# Sizing profile for a large multi-source run. Source it BEFORE the variables that
# identify your cluster:
#
#   . bin/profiles/large-run.env
#   . ~/my-cluster.env          # master URL, driver host, bucket paths -- NOT tracked
#   bin/submit_spark_job.sh --mode full ...
#
# WHAT BELONGS HERE: values consumed by bin/submit_spark_job.sh that describe how big
# the job is. Nothing here names a host, an address, a bucket, an account or a path, so
# this file is safe to track. Keep it that way -- anything that identifies a deployment
# goes in an untracked file you source afterwards.
#
# WHAT THIS IS SIZED FOR: a four-source day where one source dominates -- measured at
# 347M triples total, of which the market source is 345M (99.5%). That run reads a whole
# day of intraday snapshots rather than a single one; the difference is a directory path
# versus a file path, and it is worth ~19x. A single-snapshot run needs none of this and
# should just use the script's defaults.
#
# NOT FOR --mode pyg_only. This profile gives the executor side 94 GiB of a 121 GiB
# host, which is right for enrichment and wrong for assembly -- assembly builds the
# HeteroData in the driver's own memory and needs most of the host for it. Use
# bin/profiles/pyg-assembly.env for that leg; it carries the arithmetic.
#
# CHECK THIS BUDGET BEFORE RAISING ANYTHING. On a unified-memory GPU the RMM pool comes
# out of host RAM, so it competes with the heaps:
#
#   max_fraction x host RAM  +  executor heap  +  driver heap  +  pinned pool
#     0.35 x 121 = 42        +       48        +      16       +      4       = 110 GiB
#
# which fits the 121 GiB host these were measured on. The previous values (0.5/0.7, 64g)
# asked for 169 GiB: the OOM-killer took the executor at 67 minutes, and its
# localCheckpoint blocks went with it. Redo this math for your own host.
#
# THAT ARITHMETIC IS NOT THE WHOLE BUDGET, and 2026-08-29 is where it showed. It counts
# the pool once, as though it were a fixed reservation. It is not: cost scales with how
# many tasks are on the GPU at once. Measured by varying only the slot count --
#
#     16 concurrent tasks -> ~42 GB host RAM used
#     32 concurrent tasks -> ~58 GB
#
# -- which is ~1.0 GB per concurrent task on a ~26 GB base. At 144 tasks that is ~170 GB
# on a 121 GiB host, and two runs died proving it: one wedged both executors for ten
# minutes until a heartbeat timeout evicted one, the other drained 31.7 -> 11.7 GB in two
# and a half minutes with swap engaged.
#
# The per-task figure is not a constant. It tracks RAPIDS_BATCH_SIZE_BYTES, whose default
# is 1 GiB -- almost exactly the measured ~1.0 GB. See that setting below.

# The script's 4g default is a floor chosen to be safe on any worker -- Spark waits
# forever rather than failing when an executor request exceeds what a worker can offer,
# so the default errs small on purpose. 48g, not 64g: the executor is what the OOM-killer
# picked.
#
# The driver stays at 16g, and raising it would not have helped the 2026-08-25 run. That
# run died on the driver four times out of four, but not in its heap: toPandas() without
# Arrow hands rows to PYTHON, and the python driver process has no -Xmx. On the last leg
# it took the host into swap and the kernel killed the executor -- the largest process,
# though not the one asking for the memory. The fix for that is in the code (edge_mapper
# collects an int key in bounded chunks, not three strings in one pass); the two settings
# below are what keeps the transfer itself cheap.
export EXECUTOR_MEMORY=48g
export DRIVER_MEMORY=16g

# Move collected columns as Arrow buffers instead of one object per row. Spark's default
# is off, so this is the difference between ~20 bytes an edge and a few hundred. Left out
# of the script's defaults on purpose: it changes every toPandas() in the process, which a
# fixture or smoke run has no reason to inherit.
export PYSPARK_ARROW_ENABLED=true

# Spark's own default is 1g, which a graph this size passes on the edge-index collect
# alone. The chunk budget in edge_mapper.py bounds a single collect at roughly 400 MB, so
# this is headroom rather than a target -- if a run gets near it, the budget is the thing
# to lower.
export DRIVER_MAX_RESULT_SIZE=8g

# The script's defaults are 0.25/0.4. 0.35 is below its cap on purpose, because this host
# also carries the driver. Both must move together -- raising alloc alone cannot grow the
# pool past the cap.
#
# Raising these does NOT relieve pool pressure; measured 2026-08-29 on the same BLS source,
# same code, fractions the only difference:
#     0.25/0.35 -> pool 24,345 MB, 1,063 pool-exceeded lines
#     0.30/0.40 -> pool 29,840 MB, 1,256 pool-exceeded lines
# A 22% bigger pool took 18% MORE overflow, so the bump was reverted. What decides crowding
# is how many tasks share one pool -- GPU_PER_TASK below -- not how big the pool is.
export RAPIDS_GPU_ALLOC_FRACTION=0.25
export RAPIDS_GPU_MAX_ALLOC_FRACTION=0.35

# Task slots per executor are min(cores/task.cpus, gpu/task.gpu), so a fractional
# task.gpu is a slot cap: 0.125 gives 8, 0.25 gives 4. This was 0.25 for a long time
# because crowding a shared RMM pool produced 215,144 "maximum pool size exceeded" lines
# from one executor, and fewer slots was the only lever anyone had.
#
# 0 declares no GPU requirement, so slots fall back to the executor's cores -- 144 across
# this cluster instead of 8. That is the whole point of #342: the parse is CPU work
# scheduled as though it needed a GPU, and the cap left 136 cores idle.
#
# THIS SETTING AND RAPIDS_BATCH_SIZE_BYTES MOVE TOGETHER. Raising the slot count without
# lowering the batch size is what killed two runs on 2026-08-29 -- see the budget note at
# the top. At 256m all 144 slots held with 44 GB of host RAM still free, more headroom
# than 64 slots had at the 1 GiB default.
export GPU_PER_TASK=0
# The fraction above only caps slots while a GPU resource is declared; 0 here is what
# lets the slot count actually reach the core count.
export GPU_PER_EXECUTOR=0

# Per CONCURRENT TASK, not per executor -- which is what makes it, not the slot count,
# the setting that decides how many tasks fit in host memory on a unified-memory host.
# RAPIDS defaults it to 1g. Lower this before lowering concurrency.
export RAPIDS_BATCH_SIZE_BYTES=256m

# Host memory staged for host<->device transfer, divided among the task slots above --
# four ways at the GPU_PER_TASK set here, not eight.
export RAPIDS_PINNED_POOL=4G

# A busy executor can go quiet for longer than the stock 120s without being dead: one
# stalled for 173s inside block eviction, was evicted, and destroyed the localCheckpoint
# blocks it held -- which aborted the job with "Checkpoint block rdd_N not found" after
# 2.5 hours. Those blocks have no replica, so eviction is not recoverable mid-run.
# The trade is real: a genuinely dead executor also goes undetected for 10 minutes.
# This only helps an executor that is alive but quiet. One that dies still ends the job.
export NETWORK_TIMEOUT=600s
export EXECUTOR_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL=30s

# RAPIDS_EXPLAIN=ALL renders a plan per execution across ~1,300 stages. On the run above
# it was a material part of a 306 MB / 1.9M-line driver log, which is slow to write and
# slower to search. Turn it on deliberately when diagnosing GPU fallback, not by default.
# With NONE, multi_experiment.ipynb has no per-operator lines to count and says so; it
# reads GPU placement out of the Spark event log instead, where the plugin's operators
# are named Gpu*. It used to report a missing measurement as "NO GPU" for every leg.
export RAPIDS_EXPLAIN=NONE
